atus\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"node1's vtnet1/vtnet2 configured as opt1(lan-a, 10.60.1.1/24)/opt2(lan-b, 10.60.2.1/24) via config.xml + configctl interface reconfigure; node2 (10.60.1.2) and node3 (10.60.2.2) each reach node1's respective LAN gateway, and node2 successfully pings node3's lan-b address across node1 (confirmed also by tcpdump capture showing echo request/reply traversing vtnet1), demonstrating live cross-network routing rather than just configuration.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-46328863e80a469a\",\"cmd-685e521e2a114ae0\",\"cmd-b1e59b6e4ee44d04\",\"cmd-befe205715ff42db\",\"cmd-41a335f83e6d4ed2\",\"cmd-b190bbc9f6954dbd\",\"cmd-05bfa7a91e104952\"]},{\"id\":\"block-tcp23\",\"requirement\":\"Refuse TCP port 23 between lan-a and lan-b\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"Logged pf block rules for tcp/23 were inserted ahead of the general pass rules on both opt1 and opt2 interfaces; nc connection attempts to port 23 timed out in both directions (node2->node3 and node3->node2), while tcp/22 (ssh) between the same hosts succeeded, directly demonstrating that only telnet is refused and other traffic still passes.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-0022583a3ddd44f2\",\"cmd-2002b4df78cf4607\",\"cmd-50a13e2beb9645e0\",\"cmd-d2db30859a554e8f\",\"cmd-e9e59181aecb42fd\"]},{\"id\":\"log-refused\",\"requirement\":\"Record every refused attempt in a log on node1\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"The pf block rules carry the 'log' keyword, so OPNsense's native filter log automatically captures every blocked tcp/23 packet (confirmed via read_log.py output showing each nc attempt's SYN as a distinct block entry with a descriptive label). The executor additionally extracted these entries into a persistent /root/telnet_block.log on node1 (chosen because /var/log is tmpfs) and confirmed the file grows with each new attempt, including after node1's own reboot.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-74a6173c3ec4498c\",\"cmd-f3448c1f10ae423c\",\"cmd-828d783d027b4277\",\"cmd-7b1da708d4544ab3\",\"cmd-7c3698a298f14ece\"]},{\"id\":\"log-entry-node2\",\"requirement\":\"Show a log entry produced by an attempt made from node2\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"Log extraction output explicitly shows an entry '2026-09-05T17:59:38 vtnet1 block 10.60.1.2 -> 10.60.2.2:23 ... telnet lan-a to lan-b' where 10.60.1.2 is node2's lan-a address, directly correlating the logged block to node2's earlier nc attempt (cmd-50a13e2beb9645e0); a matching post-reboot entry was also produced from a second node2 attempt.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-50a13e2beb9645e0\",\"cmd-f3448c1f10ae423c\",\"cmd-828d783d027b4277\"]},{\"id\":\"restart-node1\",\"requirement\":\"Provider-wide restart evidence: node1 baseline, ack, changed identity, recovered state\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"Pre-restart kern.boottime (17:53:52) and healthy pf rule/log state captured; configctl system reboot dispatched and acknowledged; SSH timed out during the transition then reconnected with a new kern.boottime (18:00:30), proving an actual reboot; post-restart pf block/pass rules and interfaces were intact, and a fresh node2-originated tcp/23 block was logged and persisted, directly demonstrating recovered routing, policy, and logging state. Cross-checked against the fixed after-executor boot-identity snapshot, which matches.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-f5cb57bc07ae40b8\",\"cmd-513b87740e79422e\",\"cmd-1fe125dbb73e4b6e\",\"cmd-9f46e0aec35f425f\",\"cmd-8353cea849bd41e6\",\"cmd-a5ecad17625b4629\",\"cmd-20d06d92fc364c1a\",\"cmd-7b1da708d4544ab3\"]},{\"id\":\"restart-node2\",\"requirement\":\"Provider-wide restart evidence: node2 baseline, ack, changed identity, recovered state\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"Pre-restart boot_id (92ec88af...) captured; reboot dispatched via backgrounded 'reboot' command with an explicit acknowledgement printed before the connection would drop; post-restart boot_id (95b30093...) differs, proving a real reboot; connectivity to node1's lan-a gateway was re-verified by ping after reapplying the (expectedly ephemeral) IP. Matches the fixed after-executor snapshot.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-d1f948764e844410\",\"cmd-5400aeace8c84424\",\"cmd-d9067fb49eac47be\"]},{\"id\":\"restart-node3\",\"requirement\":\"Provider-wide restart evidence: node3 baseline, ack, changed identity, recovered state\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"Pre-restart boot_id (242af11e...) captured; reboot dispatched and acknowledged; post-restart boot_id (3b2dd459...) differs, proving a real reboot; connectivity to node1's lan-b gateway re-verified, and a final full end-to-end check (node2->node3 ping plus renewed tcp/23 block) confirmed the whole topology and policy survived all three restarts. Matches the fixed after-executor snapshot.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-96072b6ba0e54f6d\",\"cmd-748a9086b3544b8a\",\"cmd-8d42fe3a10d44a95\",\"cmd-3d6f5a4291e44f73\"]}],\"confidence\":0.85,\"operational_hygiene\":{\"score\":0.82,\"summary\":\"Most state-changing commands map clearly to a task purpose: ephemeral ifconfig IP assignment on node1 (cmd-46328863e80a469a) preceded and was superseded by the persistent config.xml edits (cmd-67e85fc519fd48e9, cmd-685e521e2a114ae0); node2/node3 ip addr/ip route additions were necessary, low-risk test-client configuration expected to be non-persistent on these simple Ubuntu hosts and were correctly redone after their own reboots (cmd-d9067fb49eac47be, cmd-8d42fe3a10d44a95). One ineffective mutation attempt occurred (cmd-513b87740e79422e's 'configctl reboot &' returned 'Action not allowed or missing' and had no effect, immediately corrected by cmd-1fe125dbb73e4b6e using the working 'configctl system reboot' invocation) — bounded and harmless. A background tcpdump diagnostic (cmd-c097e79df73b463a) hit a 120s proxy timeout; it was a bounded packet-count-limited capture (self-terminating) and a working replacement was started and properly killed afterward (cmd-5804c60253d148f5, cmd-b190bbc9f6954dbd), so any orphaned process was low-risk and self-limiting. An initial log-extraction destination (/var/log/telnet_block.log, cmd-828d783d027b4277) was found to be lost after reboot because /var/log is tmpfs; this was a re
